ustries for tapping solar energy, which would be used for air-conditioning, hot water needs and other thermal applications"</ref> MST's president, Prakash Shrivastava <ref>Malachandar, G. (16 March 2012) '[http://www.mydigitalfc.com/knowledge/renewable-energy-sector-left-unhappy-196 Renewable energy sector left unhappy] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130617074209/http://www.mydigitalfc.com/knowledge/renewable-energy-sector-left-unhappy-196 |date=17 June 2013 }},''Financial Chronicle'', accessed 7 January 2013</ref> is a member of the Solar Energy Society of India's Governing Council.<ref>{{cite web|title=Governing Council of SESI 2009–10 |publisher=Solar Energy Society of India |access-date=6 April 2010 |url=http://www.sesi.in/governingcouncil.asp |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100124070249/http://www.sesi.in/governingcouncil.asp |archive-date=24 January 2010 }}</ref> According to a press release published in [[Reuters]], MST was included in the  "2011–2015 Deep Research Report on Chinese Solar Grade Polysilicon Industry".<ref>Unknown author (6 January 2012) {{usurped|1=[https://web.archive.org/web/20120305224906/http://www.reuters.com/article/2012/01/06/idUS92512+06-Jan-2012+BW20120106 Research and Markets: 2011-2015 Deep Research Report on Chinese Solar Grade Polysilicon Industry]}} Reuters, accessed 7 January 2013</ref>

